hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grid contains two colour‑2 brackets that face each other, plus two colour‑5 shapes outside them. In the output the brackets stay put and each 5‑shape ends up inside the opening.\n\n**Where each shape goes**\n- The new position is not a simple slide. In pair 1, the right shape is a column of four with a two‑cell bump on the right. In the output the bump is on the left, so the shape has been mirrored.\n- Pair 3 shows the same flip for both of its shapes.\n- The mirror line is the bracket's outer edge, the line of the bracket nearest the shape:\n  - Pair 1, left: x=0,1 → 6,5, so the axis is x=3, the bracket's left column.\n  - Pair 1, right: x=13,14 → 9,8, so the axis is x=11, the bracket's right column.\n  - Pair 2, top: y=0,1 → 6,5, so the axis is y=3.\n  - Pair 2, bottom: y=13 → 9, so the axis is y=11.\n\n**Rule**\n1. For each 5‑object, find the nearest 2‑object (its bracket).\n2. Decide which side of the bracket the shape lies on: left, right, above or below.\n3. Reflect every cell of the shape across the bracket line on that side. For a shape to the left the axis is x = bracket x0, so a cell's new x is 2·x0 − x. The other three sides work the same way with x1, y0 or y1.\n4. Erase the original cells and paint the reflected ones.\n\n**Test**\n- The top shape (rows 0–1) reflects across y=3 into rows 6 and 5, so it appears upside down just under the top bracket.\n- The bottom shape (rows 13–14) reflects across y=11 into rows 9 and 8.\n\nThe program emits one stage that erases the outside shapes, then one stage per reflected shape.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, diagonal=True)\n    colours = {}\n    for o in objs:\n        colours.setdefault(o[\"colour\"], []).append(o)\n    # bracket colour: the one whose objects are larger in total box; movers = other colour\n    # identify brackets as the colour with bigger total size\n    cols = sorted(colours, key=lambda c: -sum(o[\"size\"] for o in colours[c]))\n    brk_c = cols[0]\n    brackets = colours[brk_c]\n    movers = [o for c in cols[1:] for o in colours[c]]\n    out = g.copy()\n    for m in movers:\n        for (y, x) in m[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y, x] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase the shapes lying outside the brackets.\")\n    H, W = g.shape\n    for m in movers:\n        my0, mx0, my1, mx1 = m[\"box\"]\n        cy, cx = (my0 + my1) / 2, (mx0 + mx1) / 2\n        def dist(b):\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = b[\"box\"]\n            return abs((y0 + y1) / 2 - cy) + abs((x0 + x1) / 2 - cx)\n        b = min(brackets, key=dist)\n        by0, bx0, by1, bx1 = b[\"box\"]\n        new = []\n        for (y, x) in m[\"cells\"]:\n            if mx1 < bx0:\n                ny, nx = y, 2 * bx0 - x\n            elif mx0 > bx1:\n                ny, nx = y, 2 * bx1 - x\n            elif my1 < by0:\n                ny, nx = 2 * by0 - y, x\n            else:\n                ny, nx = 2 * by1 - y, x\n            new.append((ny, nx))\n        for (ny, nx) in new:\n            if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W:\n                out[ny, nx] = m[\"colour\"]\n        emit(out, \"Mirror a shape across the outer edge of its nearest bracket, placing it inside.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In the output the bump is on the left, so the shape has been mirrored."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The mirror line is the bracket's outer edge, the line of the bracket nearest the shape:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Reflect every cell of the shape across the bracket line on that side."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"64d59017afa1ca3fc287a2662813dee4d8312a848419a129b53c33358ee57d73","task_sha256":"e8a54f83c166a62b2cbdc5362bf7a10143a4141533416c6c835a177a75a18e60","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
